CAVEAT: the disk-read numbers above are almost certainly invalid -- these
layer files were written to disk minutes earlier by a prior run, and macOS's
page cache serves recently-written files from RAM. 731MB reading in ~1ms
implies ~700GB/s throughput, which is physically impossible for real storage.
This session has no sudo access to force a true cache flush (`purge`), so a
clean cold-read measurement isn't possible here directly.

Cross-check instead, using the real full run's actual measured timing
(run_output.log: 2816.5s generating 40 tokens, 64 layers/token):
  avg combined (disk read + compute) time per layer, from the real run: 1100.2 ms
  measured FFN compute time (real weights, batch=1): 16.9 ms
  implied REAL (cold) disk read time per layer: 1083.3 ms
  disk read is ~64x longer than compute per layer

Conclusion: prefetching hides compute-time-worth of disk latency behind
the PREVIOUS layer's compute. With disk read ~64x longer than compute per
layer, even 1-layer-ahead prefetching can only hide a tiny fraction of the
read -- the disk is already the bottleneck and stays continuously busy.
A deeper prefetch queue (3 layers instead of 2) cannot speed up a disk
that's already saturated; it would only queue MORE reads ahead of a pipe
that's already the limiting factor, not read faster.
